
# LINUX BASIC AND IMPORTANT COMMANDS

## 1. Directory and Navigation Commands

| Command | Description                                              | Example        |
| ------- | -------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- |
| `pwd`   | Displays the current working directory.                  | `pwd`          |
| `ls`    | Lists files and directories.                             | `ls`           |
| `ls -l` | Displays files with detailed information.                | `ls -l`        |
| `ls -a` | Displays all files, including hidden files.              | `ls -a`        |
| `cd`    | Changes the current directory.                           | `cd Documents` |
| `cd ..` | Moves to the parent directory.                           | `cd ..`        |
| `cd ~`  | Moves to the user's home directory.                      | `cd ~`         |
| `cd /`  | Moves to the root directory.                             | `cd /`         |
| `tree`  | Displays directories in a tree structure (if installed). | `tree`         |

## 2. File and Directory Management Commands

| Command    | Description                                        | Example                |
| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- |
| `touch`    | Creates an empty file if it does not exist.        | `touch file.txt`       |
| `mkdir`    | Creates a new directory.                           | `mkdir project`        |
| `mkdir -p` | Creates nested directories as needed.              | `mkdir -p project/src` |
| `rmdir`    | Removes an empty directory.                        | `rmdir project`        |
| `cp`       | Copies files.                                      | `cp a.txt b.txt`       |
| `cp -r`    | Copies directories and their contents recursively. | `cp -r src backup`     |
| `mv`       | Moves or renames files and directories.            | `mv old.txt new.txt`   |
| `rm`       | Removes a file.                                    | `rm file.txt`          |
| `rm -r`    | Removes a directory and its contents recursively.  | `rm -r backup`         |
| `file`     | Identifies a file's type.                          | `file program.c`       |

Important: Be careful with `rm`, especially `rm -r`. Deleted files may not be recoverable.

## 3. File Viewing and Text Processing Commands

| Command     | Description                                      | Example                |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------- |
| `cat`       | Displays file contents.                          | `cat notes.txt`        |
| `less`      | Views a file one screen at a time.               | `less notes.txt`       |
| `more`      | Displays file contents page by page.             | `more notes.txt`       |
| `head`      | Displays the first 10 lines by default.          | `head notes.txt`       |
| `head -n 5` | Displays the first 5 lines.                      | `head -n 5 notes.txt`  |
| `tail`      | Displays the last 10 lines by default.           | `tail notes.txt`       |
| `tail -f`   | Continuously displays new lines added to a file. | `tail -f app.log`      |
| `wc`        | Counts lines, words, and bytes.                  | `wc notes.txt`         |
| `sort`      | Sorts lines of text.                             | `sort names.txt`       |
| `uniq`      | Removes adjacent duplicate lines.                | `uniq names.txt`       |
| `grep`      | Searches for matching text.                      | `grep "error" app.log` |
| `diff`      | Compares two files line by line.                 | `diff a.txt b.txt`     |

## 4. File Permissions and Ownership Commands

| Command    | Description                                       | Example                    |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------- |
| `chmod`    | Changes file permissions.                         | `chmod 755 script.sh`      |
| `chmod +x` | Adds execute permission.                          | `chmod +x script.sh`       |
| `chown`    | Changes file owner.                               | `sudo chown user file.txt` |
| `ls -l`    | Displays file permissions and ownership.          | `ls -l`                    |
| `umask`    | Displays or sets default permission restrictions. | `umask`                    |

Linux permissions are represented by `r` (read), `w` (write), and `x` (execute).

Example: `chmod 755 script.sh` gives the owner read, write, and execute permissions, while the group and others get read and execute permissions.

