| Command    | Description                                      | Example    |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ---------- |
| `whoami`   | Displays the current username.                   | `whoami`   |
| `who`      | Displays logged-in users.                        | `who`      |
| `id`       | Displays user ID and group information.          | `id`       |
| `hostname` | Displays the system's hostname.                  | `hostname` |
| `uname`    | Displays kernel information.                     | `uname`    |
| `uname -a` | Displays detailed system and kernel information. | `uname -a` |
| `date`     | Displays the current date and time.              | `date`     |
| `uptime`   | Displays how long the system has been running.   | `uptime`   |
| `history`  | Displays previously executed commands.           | `history`  |
| `clear`    | Clears the terminal screen.                      | `clear`    |

## 6. Process Management Commands

| Command   | Description                                           | Example        |
| --------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | -------------- |
| `ps`      | Displays processes associated with the terminal.      | `ps`           |
| `ps aux`  | Displays a detailed list of running processes.        | `ps aux`       |
| `top`     | Displays live process and resource usage information. | `top`          |
| `kill`    | Sends a signal to a process using its PID.            | `kill 1234`    |
| `kill -9` | Forcefully terminates a process.                      | `kill -9 1234` |
| `pgrep`   | Finds process IDs by process name.                    | `pgrep python` |
| `jobs`    | Lists jobs started in the current shell.              | `jobs`         |
| `bg`      | Resumes a stopped job in the background.              | `bg`           |
| `fg`      | Brings a background job to the foreground.            | `fg`           |
| `sleep`   | Pauses execution for a specified duration.            | `sleep 5`      |

Note: Replace `1234` with the actual process ID. Use `kill -9` only when normal termination does not work.

## 7. System Monitoring and Disk Commands

| Command   | Description                                          | Example            |
| --------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |
| `df -h`   | Displays available and used disk space.              | `df -h`            |
| `du -sh`  | Displays the total size of a directory or file.      | `du -sh Documents` |
| `free -h` | Displays memory usage.                               | `free -h`          |
| `lsblk`   | Lists block storage devices and partitions.          | `lsblk`            |
| `blkid`   | Displays block device identification information.    | `sudo blkid`       |
| `mount`   | Displays mounted filesystems or mounts a filesystem. | `mount`            |
| `dmesg`   | Displays kernel messages.                            | `dmesg`            |
| `uptime`  | Displays uptime and system load averages.            | `uptime`           |
| `lscpu`   | Displays CPU architecture information.               | `lscpu`            |
| `lsusb`   | Lists USB devices, if the utility is installed.      | `lsusb`            |

## 8. Networking Commands

| Command       | Description                                            | Example                                      |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |
| `ip addr`     | Displays network interfaces and IP addresses.          | `ip addr`                                    |
| `ip link`     | Displays network interface status.                     | `ip link`                                    |
| `ip route`    | Displays routing information.                          | `ip route`                                   |
| `ping`        | Tests network connectivity.                            | `ping -c 4 google.com`                       |
| `hostname -I` | Displays assigned IP addresses.                        | `hostname -I`                                |
